// XFAIL: RT-501: a level difference, not unsound: x86-64 at -O0 refuses a local `[7u8; 1usize << 30]` (1 GiB) with frame.too_big ("reaches 2 GiB less a page"): gen0 builds the repeat array in a temporary slot and copies it, so the frame holds it twice; -O2 builds the same program, and so does rustc at every level; fixed, gen0 builds the repeat in place and -O0 does what -O2 does (0.30.2)
// From the g302frame review. Since v0.30.1 at least: v0.30.1's -O0 is
// an internal assembler error ("an immediate a 64-bit operation cannot
// sign-extend from 32 bits: subq $2147484288, %rsp"), its -O2 builds.
// aarch64 builds it at both levels. Built, the frame of 1 GiB is probed
// as `one` is entered and faults on the guard page after "before"
// (stack_probe_frame_past_limit's design; rustc without -O builds it
// for x86-64 too and aborts there on the stack overflow, exit 134). The
// case builds for x86_64-macos whatever the runner's target and runs
// under Rosetta on an arm Mac: REQUIRES: macos.
// REQUIRES: macos
// FLAGS: -O0 --target x86_64-macos
// EXPECT: before
// EXIT: 139
#[inline(never)]
fn one(n: usize) -> u8 {
    if n > 5 {
        let a = [7u8; 1usize << 30];
        return a[n];
    }
    println("inside");
    0
}

fn main() {
    let n = std::env::args().count();
    println("before");
    println("{}", one(n));
}
